.TH PAM_HOWDY 8 "@HOWDY_MAN_DATE@" "Howdy Next @PROJECT_VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_howdy \- PAM facial authentication module for Howdy Next
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam_howdy.so
[\fBworkaround=\fR\fIvalue\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_howdy.so
authenticates a PAM user with Howdy Next facial recognition.
.PP
The module reads the configured Howdy file at
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
and enrolled user models from
.IR @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@ .
These paths are expected to remain root-controlled and non-user-writable.
.SH OPTIONS
The
.B workaround=
option is service-local. It is configured on the
.B pam_howdy.so
line and is not read from
.IR config.ini .
Omit the option for the default mode.
@PAM_WORKAROUND_REFERENCE@
.SH "WORKAROUND BEHAVIOR"
The default is off when
.B workaround=
is omitted. Empty, unknown, or malformed values, including
.B workaround=off ,
also select the off behavior. Only the first argument beginning with
.B workaround=
is considered; later duplicate arguments do not override it.
.PP
.B workaround=input
is available when Howdy is configured to ask for an authentication token and no
PAM token already exists. Howdy checks access to
.IR /dev/uinput ,
installs a prompt observer, and may send one synthetic Enter keypress after a
successful face comparison while a hidden-input prompt is active. Setup or
preflight failure disables this workaround and leaves the standard PAM prompt in
place. A failed or ineffective keypress does not complete authentication; finish
the password prompt manually.
.PP
.B workaround=native
requires a usable terminal. The
.B PAM_TTY
item must identify an open terminal whose foreground process group is the current
process group. At least one of standard input, standard output, or standard error
must be an open terminal for that same device; any of the other standard
descriptors may be redirected. A graphical conversation may provide TTY metadata
without providing matching standard-terminal descriptors, so native handling is
unavailable in that case. The native mode does not silently fall back to input
injection when terminal setup or conversation installation fails; it disables the
workaround and uses the normal PAM conversation instead.
.PP
.B workaround=native-input
tries native handling first. If native handling is unavailable or cannot be
installed, it falls back to the input behavior described above. If native handling
succeeds, input injection is not also enabled.
.PP
Native and input prompt conversations are restored after the authentication run.
If restoration cannot restore the original conversation, Howdy installs a
fail-closed conversation where possible and returns a system error; an unsafe
restoration also leaves the callback context quarantined. An existing PAM
authentication token prevents either workaround from starting.
.PP
Input mode requires access to
.IR /dev/uinput .
The synthetic key is delivered to the currently focused input device, not to a
specific PAM window, and can reach another application. Enable input injection
only for a service and display environment whose focus and device behavior have
been validated.
.SH SETUP
.SS Basic
For a PAM consumer where Howdy should run as a sufficient authentication method:
.PP
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_howdy.so
.fi
.SS TTY, Login, And Sudo
For TTY-backed services that call PAM before collecting a password, put Howdy
before
.BR pam_unix (8).
Use
.B workaround=native
if Howdy should run concurrently with the password prompt and stop that prompt
after successful face authentication:
.PP
.nf
auth  optional     pam_exec.so /usr/bin/linux-enable-ir-emitter run --config /etc/linux-enable-ir-emitter.toml
auth  sufficient   pam_howdy.so workaround=native
auth  sufficient   pam_unix.so try_first_pass nullok
auth  sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
.fi
.SS Graphical Authorization Prompts
Graphical PAM consumers, including graphical Polkit prompts, may not provide a
usable TTY. If Howdy must stop a concurrent password prompt, use
.B workaround=native-input
to prefer native prompt handling where available and fall back to input
injection otherwise:
.PP
.nf
auth  sufficient   pam_howdy.so workaround=native-input
.fi
.PP
The input fallback sends Enter through
.IR /dev/uinput .
It sends at most once after Howdy observes a PAM hidden-input password prompt.
This observation does not prove graphical readiness or keyboard focus. Enter is
not targeted to the PAM prompt and may instead reach another application that
currently has keyboard focus. If ineffective, complete password entry manually.
Validate this behavior for each graphical consumer before enabling it.
.PP
Howdy will not begin a new synthetic Enter attempt after the password request has
returned. Howdy sends no more than one Enter attempt.
.SS Lock Screens That Collect Input Before PAM
Some lock screens, including
.BR waylock ,
collect password input before calling PAM. For those services, validate the
already-submitted password first, then fall through to Howdy and fingerprint
authentication:
.PP
.nf
auth  optional     pam_exec.so /usr/bin/linux-enable-ir-emitter run --config /etc/linux-enable-ir-emitter.toml
auth  sufficient   pam_unix.so try_first_pass nullok
auth  sufficient   pam_howdy.so
auth  sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
.fi
.PP
When a PAM authentication token already exists,
.B pam_howdy.so
does not start the native or input workaround, even if a workaround option is
configured.
.PP
If Howdy cannot restore original PAM conversation after workaround use,
authentication fails with system error.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Do not make
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_DIR_ROFF@
or
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
world-readable to support unprivileged lock screens. Howdy Next expects the
protected source config and model paths to remain root-controlled.
.PP
PAM consumers that run authentication as the regular user use the installed
.B howdy-auth-helper
setuid helper to prepare temporary root-controlled runtime copies of the
protected config and enrolled model before recognition.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
Main Howdy configuration file.
.TP
.IR @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
Configured directory containing enrolled user face models.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_AUTH_HELPER_PATH_ROFF@
Setuid helper used for unprivileged PAM consumers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR howdy (1),
.BR howdy.ini (5),
.BR pam (8),
.BR pam_unix (8),
.BR pam_fprintd (8)
